      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Disaster recovery fails quietly. The plan is signed and filed. Then the standby site drifts. Production gets an upgrade the DR kit never sees. A circuit gets borrowed. Batteries age. A walk catches it while you can still fix it. Here is the checklist.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A DR site audit is a walk that shows if the standby site can carry the load.</li><li>Check power, cooling, kit, links and access.</li><li>The key test: does the room match the plan?</li><li>ISO 22301 is the business continuity standard. Your walk gives it evidence.</li><li>Quickler records the walk and the photos. It does not run the failover test.</li><li>You judge if the site is ready. Quickler does not.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Context",
      "heading": "What you are checking",
      "html": "<p>A DR site is the standby place your systems move to if the main site fails. It may be a second data centre or a rack in someone else's building.</p><p>The risk is drift. A site that was right on day one slowly goes wrong. Your walk shows if it still meets the recovery targets the business set.</p>"
    },
